1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,780
This is MATH 239, video 26 b. It is part 2 of matchings, covers and König's Theorem.

2
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:30,032
Here are the definitions from part 1. G is bipartite with bipartition A, B, and M is a matching. X 0 is the set of vertices of A that M does not saturate. Z is the set of vertices joined to X 0 by an alternating path, P of v. X is A intersect Z, and Y is B intersect Z.

3
00:00:30,482 --> 00:00:37,448
After this video, you can prove König's Theorem. You can use it in a proof. And you can run the matching algorithm and write the answer.

4
00:00:37,898 --> 00:00:48,022
Look at an alternating path from X 0. It starts in A, with an edge not in M. Then the edges alternate, and the vertices alternate between A and B.

5
00:00:48,472 --> 00:00:57,342
So a path to a vertex of X has even length, and its last edge is in M. If v is in X 0, the path has no edges, and there is nothing to check.

6
00:00:57,792 --> 00:01:02,529
A path to a vertex of Y has odd length, and its last edge is not in M.

7
00:01:02,979 --> 00:01:11,849
One more fact. Each vertex w on a path P of v is itself joined to X 0 by the first part of that path. So w is in Z.

8
00:01:12,299 --> 00:01:21,030
Lemma 8.3.2 is the core of the proof. The figure splits A into X and A minus X, and B into Y and B minus Y.

9
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:25,056
Part a: no edge of G joins X to B minus Y.

10
00:01:25,506 --> 00:01:32,704
Part b: the set C, equal to Y union A minus X, is a cover. It is the green outline.

11
00:01:33,154 --> 00:01:37,194
Part c: no edge of M joins Y to A minus X.

12
00:01:37,644 --> 00:01:47,629
Part d: the size of M equals the size of C minus the size of U. Here U is the set of vertices of Y that M does not saturate.

13
00:01:48,079 --> 00:01:52,816
Part e: for each vertex of U, there is an augmenting path to it.

14
00:01:53,266 --> 00:02:10,913
Proof of part a. Suppose u is in X, v is in B minus Y, and u v is an edge. The path P of u has even length. Its last edge is the matching edge at u, or u is in X 0 and has no matching edge. In both cases, u v is not in M.

15
00:02:11,363 --> 00:02:26,966
So we can add v to the end of P of u. v is not on the path, because the path is inside Z. The result is an alternating path of odd length to v. So v is in Y. This contradicts v in B minus Y.

16
00:02:27,416 --> 00:02:40,187
Part b. Every edge joins A to B. An edge with no end in C has its A end in X and its B end in B minus Y. By part a, no such edge exists. So C is a cover.

17
00:02:40,637 --> 00:02:58,517
Part c. Suppose u v is in M, with u in Y and v in A minus X. P of u has odd length, and its last edge is not in M. Add the matching edge u v. We get an alternating path of even length to v. So v is in X. Contradiction.

18
00:02:58,967 --> 00:03:14,246
Part d. An edge of M that starts in X must end in Y, by part a. An edge of M that starts in Y must end in X, by part c. So each edge of M joins Y to X, or joins A minus X to B minus Y.

19
00:03:14,696 --> 00:03:24,123
Count the first type. Each saturated vertex of Y has exactly one edge of M, and it goes to X. So there are Y minus U edges of this type.

20
00:03:24,573 --> 00:03:36,926
Count the second type. X 0 is inside X, so every vertex of A minus X is saturated. Its matching edge goes to B minus Y. So there are A minus X edges of this type.

21
00:03:37,376 --> 00:03:45,085
Add the two counts. The size of M is Y minus U plus A minus X. That is the size of C minus the size of U.

22
00:03:45,535 --> 00:03:56,402
Part e. If v is in U, then P of v starts at a vertex of X 0 and ends at v. Neither end is saturated. So P of v is an augmenting path.

23
00:03:56,852 --> 00:04:13,152
Check the lemma on the first example. Y is all of B, and A minus X is empty. The vertices g and j are not saturated, so U is g, j. Part d says 4 equals 6 minus 2 plus 0. That is true.

24
00:04:13,602 --> 00:04:22,097
Part e says that there are augmenting paths to g and to j. Two of them are b i c g and b i c j.

25
00:04:22,547 --> 00:04:29,791
Use the path b i c j. Swap its edges: remove c i from M, and add b i and c j.

26
00:04:30,241 --> 00:04:42,687
The result is the matching of Figure 8.4. Now U is empty, Y is i, l, and A minus X is a, c, e. Part d says 5 equals 2 minus 0 plus 3.

27
00:04:43,137 --> 00:04:52,379
So C is i, l, a, c, e. It is a cover with 5 vertices, the same size as M. This is the cover that we used before.

28
00:04:52,829 --> 00:04:58,169
Now the proof of König's Theorem. Let M be a maximum matching of the bipartite graph G.

29
00:04:58,619 --> 00:05:09,793
By Lemma 8.1.1, a maximum matching has no augmenting path. By part e, each vertex of U has an augmenting path. So U is empty.

30
00:05:10,243 --> 00:05:19,995
By part b, C equal to Y union A minus X is a cover. By part d, its size is the size of M minus 0.

31
00:05:20,445 --> 00:05:34,517
So M and C have the same size. By Lemma 8.2.2, C is a minimum cover. So the maximum size of a matching equals the minimum size of a cover. This completes the proof.

32
00:05:34,967 --> 00:05:49,595
Here is Problem 8.3.3 of the notes. G is bipartite, both sides have n vertices, and G has q edges. Prove that G has a matching of size at least q over n. Pause the video and try it.

33
00:05:50,045 --> 00:06:00,157
We need the maximum size of a matching. By König's Theorem, it equals the minimum size of a cover. So it is enough to show that every cover has at least q over n vertices.

34
00:06:00,607 --> 00:06:15,700
Take a cover C. The other side has n vertices, so each vertex of C has at most n edges. So at most n times C edges meet C. In the example, n is 4. The colours show the edges of each cover vertex.

35
00:06:16,150 --> 00:06:27,481
C is a cover, so every one of the q edges meets C. So n times C is at least q, and C is at least q over n. This is true for every cover, so it is true for the minimum cover.

36
00:06:27,931 --> 00:06:43,953
In the example, n is 4 and q is 10. So there is a matching of size at least 2.5, so at least 3. This graph has a matching of size 3. A brute force check of all bipartite graphs with n equal to 3 also agrees.

37
00:06:44,403 --> 00:06:57,684
The XY construction also gives an algorithm. Step 0: start with any matching M. Step 1: X hat is the set of unsaturated vertices of A. Y hat is empty. No vertex has a parent yet.

38
00:06:58,134 --> 00:07:08,491
Step 2: for each vertex v of B, not yet in Y hat, that has an edge to some u in X hat: add v to Y hat, and set the parent of v to u.

39
00:07:08,941 --> 00:07:18,368
Step 3: if step 2 added nothing, stop. M is a maximum matching, and Y hat union A minus X hat is a minimum cover.

40
00:07:18,818 --> 00:07:31,078
Step 4: if step 2 added an unsaturated vertex v, follow the parents back from v to X hat. This is an augmenting path. Swap its edges to get a larger matching. Then go back to step 1.

41
00:07:31,528 --> 00:07:43,974
Step 5: for each vertex v of A, not in X hat, whose matching edge goes to some u in Y hat: add v to X hat, with parent u. Then go to step 2.

42
00:07:44,424 --> 00:08:00,721
Here is the example of the notes, Figure 8.5. A is 1, 2, 3, 4. M has the two edges 2 6 and 3 7. Step 1: the unsaturated vertices of A are 1 and 4. So X hat is 1, 4.

43
00:08:01,171 --> 00:08:09,716
Step 2: from 1 and 4, we reach 7. Its parent is 1. The vertex 7 is saturated, so we go to step 5.

44
00:08:10,166 --> 00:08:16,714
Step 5: the matching edge at 7 goes to 3. Add 3 to X hat, with parent 7.

45
00:08:17,164 --> 00:08:24,594
Step 2 again: from 3 we reach 5 and 8. Both get parent 3. Both are unsaturated.

46
00:08:25,044 --> 00:08:41,391
Step 4: take 5. Follow the parents: 5, 3, 7, 1. This is an augmenting path. Swap its edges. The new matching is 2 6, 3 5 and 1 7. It has 3 edges.

47
00:08:41,841 --> 00:08:48,389
Round 2 starts from step 1 with no parents. Only 4 is unsaturated in A. So X hat is 4.

48
00:08:48,839 --> 00:08:53,651
Step 2: from 4 we reach 7, with parent 4. It is saturated.

49
00:08:54,101 --> 00:08:59,999
Step 5: the matching edge at 7 now goes to 1. Add 1 to X hat, with parent 7.

50
00:09:00,449 --> 00:09:15,847
Step 2: the only neighbour of 1 and 4 is 7, which is already in Y hat. Nothing is added. Step 3: stop. The cover is A minus X hat, which is 2 and 3, union Y hat, which is 7.

51
00:09:16,297 --> 00:09:27,071
Check it. Every edge has an end in 2, 3 or 7. The cover and the matching both have size 3. So the matching is maximum, by Lemma 8.2.2.

52
00:09:27,521 --> 00:09:43,171
Now a practice question. It is Problem Set 8.3 number 5 in the notes. Find a maximum matching and a minimum cover of Figure 8.6, with the algorithm. Start with the matching 3 b, 4 d, 5 e. Pause the video and try it.

53
00:09:43,621 --> 00:09:47,522
Step 1: the unsaturated vertices of A are 1 and 2.

54
00:09:47,972 --> 00:09:55,857
Step 2: from 1 we reach b, with parent 1. From 2 we reach e, with parent 2. Both are saturated.

55
00:09:56,307 --> 00:10:04,063
Step 5: the matching edge at b goes to 3. The matching edge at e goes to 5. Add 3 and 5 to X hat.

56
00:10:04,513 --> 00:10:12,732
Step 2: from 3 we reach a, c and d, all with parent 3. The vertices a and c are not saturated.

57
00:10:13,182 --> 00:10:25,326
Step 4: take a. Follow the parents: a, 3, b, 1. Swap the edges. The new matching is 1 b, 3 a, 4 d, 5 e.

58
00:10:25,776 --> 00:10:30,524
Round 2. Only 2 is unsaturated in A. So X hat is 2.

59
00:10:30,974 --> 00:10:35,803
Step 2: from 2 we reach b and e. Both are saturated.

60
00:10:36,253 --> 00:10:44,009
Step 5: the matching edge at b goes to 1, and the matching edge at e goes to 5. Add 1 and 5 to X hat.

61
00:10:44,459 --> 00:10:56,766
Step 2: the neighbours of 2, 1 and 5 are only b and e. Nothing new. Stop. A minus X hat is 3 and 4. Y hat is b and e. So the cover is 3, 4, b, e.

62
00:10:57,216 --> 00:11:09,801
On a test, write the answer with a reason. First, state the matching: 1 b, 3 a, 4 d and 5 e. Its ends are all different, so it is a matching.

63
00:11:10,251 --> 00:11:21,257
Second, state the cover: 3, 4, b and e. Show that it is a cover. List the edges at each vertex. Together they are all 12 edges.

64
00:11:21,707 --> 00:11:33,874
Third, the matching and the cover both have size 4. By Lemma 8.2.2, the matching is maximum and the cover is minimum. You do not need to show the steps of the algorithm, but they help the marker.

65
00:11:34,324 --> 00:11:47,049
One more thing. The last X hat is 2, 1, 5. These three vertices have only two neighbours, b and e. So no matching can saturate all of A. The next video turns this into Hall's Theorem.

66
00:11:47,499 --> 00:11:56,844
To recap. A matching is a set of edges with no common ends. A cover is a set of vertices that meets every edge. An augmenting path gives a larger matching.

67
00:11:57,294 --> 00:12:09,786
A matching is never larger than a cover. If they have the same size, both are optimal. König's Theorem says that in a bipartite graph, the maximum matching and the minimum cover always have the same size.

68
00:12:10,236 --> 00:12:22,682
The XY construction gives the proof and the algorithm. The algorithm ends with a maximum matching and a minimum cover of the same size. The next video covers Hall's Theorem, perfect matchings and edge colouring.
